{
  "index": 9,
  "text": "There are a few Nepalese studies 13, 14 in dementia care and management, but there are no national and community-based data indicating the prevalence of dementia in Nepal 15. Despite a government policy (Geriatric Health Service Strategy) available for older people\u2019s health 16, very limited health facilities are available there. Nearly two-thirds of Nepal\u2019s older adults who rely on primary health care centres (PHCCs) for health services lack basic screening facilities for dementia. A study highlighted the need for community screening procedures for early identification 13. The Nepalese health system is not fully equipped to deal with or manage the burden of dementia care, and the exact prevalence of dementia is unknown in Nepal. Therefore, the present study is the first of its kind in Nepal to assess the prevalence of suspected cognitive impairment or to assess the risk of dementia associated with geographical location (as a proxy for socio-economic and educational capital), gender, and age, which is key to ensure adequate public health policy is in place to support people suffering from this condition.",
  "style": "Normal",
  "is_heading": false
}

