  "text": "The prevalence of cognitive impairment or risk of dementia increases monotonically with age in the present study, reflecting a significant age effect. There is evidence in the literature highlighting the increasing risk of dementia with age 34, 37. Therefore, community-level screening could be one of the best strategies to recognise the risk of dementia as early as possible and it might be beneficial to develop early interventions to reduce the risk. Using new technologies that help to identify biomarkers may be useful in the early diagnosis, prevention, and treatment of cognitive decline 38, 39. The global action plan for a public health response to dementia in the period 2017\u20132025 also describes goals and suggested courses of action to help nations handle the expanding global dementia crisis 40. According to the action plan, universal access to care and support for individuals with dementia and their carers depends on prompt and accurate dementia diagnosis, ideally provided at the primary care level. Literature has suggested being cognitively, physically, and socially active from midlife help to reduce the risk of dementia 35. The awareness programme should focus on risk factors of dementia, recognising early signs and symptoms of dementia and promoting healthy lifestyles 35, 40. Understanding the significance of cardiovascular risk factors that increase the risk of dementia is also important 8, 38; as is the ability to recognise early signs

